    {
      "filename": "A real ostrich, rubberized after death (20128495004).jpg",
      "title": "File:A real ostrich, rubberized after death (20128495004).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b6/A_real_ostrich%2C_rubberized_after_death_%2820128495004%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_real_ostrich,_rubberized_after_death_(20128495004).jpg",
      "width": 3888,
      "height": 2592,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "shankar s. from Dubai, united arab emirates",
      "description": "The real squid rubberized and preserved had fascinated me enough, but I was hardly prepared for this! At least with the squid, one couldn't see the muscles and flesh tissues. Hhere in this rubberized Ostrich, it's all there for you to see. Yes, that was once a real, living ostrich. Injecting silicone inside it has preserved all it's muscles and tissues. Bright spotlighting has over exposed the picture somewhet, sorry about that. Notes about rubberizing animal bodies appear in previous captions. (Ottawa, Canada, June 2015)",
      "dateCreated": "2015-06-01 00:00",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "canadian-museum-of-nature-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "f244",
      "mediumPath": "_m/f/2/4/4/canadian-museum-of-nature-wp/a-real-ostrich-rubberized-afte-medium.jpg",
      "smallPath": "_m/f/2/4/4/canadian-museum-of-nature-wp/a-real-ostrich-rubberized-afte-small.jpg"
    },
    {
